**user**:
> When you're performing a bootstrap for a panel regression, should you resample only the units, or only the years within each unit, or both?
**assistant**:
> It depends on what dependence structure you need the bootstrap to preserve. For a standard panel regression, the safest default is usually:
> 
> **Resample whole units, not individual observations or years within each unit.**
> 
> That is, draw units/clusters with replacement and keep each selected unit’s full time series intact. This is the usual **cluster bootstrap by unit**. It preserves within-unit serial correlation, heteroskedasticity, and the panel structure.
> 
> Use cases:
> 
> - **If units are independent but observations within a unit are correlated:**  
>   Resample **units**. This is the common choice for panel data.
> 
> - **If years have common shocks that correlate all units in the same year:**  
>   Resampling only units may understate uncertainty. Consider **clustering by year**, **two-way cluster-robust inference**, or a **two-way/multiway bootstrap**.
> 
> - **If both unit-level serial dependence and year-level common shocks matter:**  
>   Use methods designed for two-way dependence, e.g. **two-way cluster bootstrap**, **wild cluster bootstrap**, or inference based on **two-way clustered standard errors**.
> 
> - **If the time dimension is long and serial dependence is central:**  
>   You might use a **block bootstrap over time**, often within units or with blocks that preserve temporal dependence.
> 
> - **Avoid resampling years independently within each unit** unless you are confident there is no meaningful serial dependence. It scrambles each unit’s time series and can break lag structures, trends, autocorrelation, treatment timing, and fixed-effect dynamics.
> 
> So, in shorthand:
> 
> \[
> \text{Default panel bootstrap: resample units, keep all years for each sampled unit.}
> \]
> 
> If there is strong cross-sectional dependence by year, then resampling only units is not enough; use a method that accounts for **both unit and time clustering**. For many applied panel regressions with unit fixed effects and serial correlation, I would start with **cluster bootstrap by unit** or **cluster-robust SEs by unit**, then check robustness to two-way clustering if common time shocks are plausible.
> 
> For a panel regression, the usual bootstrap choice is:
> 
> > **Resample the cross-sectional units, keeping each selected unit’s full time series intact.**
> 
> So if your panel is individuals, firms, countries, counties, etc., you draw those units with replacement and include all years for each drawn unit. This is the standard **cluster bootstrap by unit**.
> 
> Why? Because panel observations within a unit are usually correlated over time. If you resample years independently within each unit, you often destroy:
> 
> - serial correlation,
> - lag structures,
> - treatment timing,
> - unit-specific trends,
> - dynamic relationships,
> - within-unit dependence induced by fixed effects.
> 
> So, as a default:
> 
> \[
> \text{bootstrap sample} = \{i_1, i_2, \dots, i_N\}, \quad i_j \sim \text{Uniform}\{1,\dots,N\},
> \]
> 
> and for each selected unit $i_j$, include all years $t=1,\dots,T$.
> 
> However, the right answer depends on the dependence structure:
> 
> 1. **Within-unit correlation only**  
>    Resample **units**. This is the common panel bootstrap.
> 
> 2. **Common shocks by year**  
>    If all units in a given year are affected by shared shocks, use **two-way clustering** or a bootstrap that accounts for both unit and year dependence.
> 
> 3. **Long time series with serial dependence as the main concern**  
>    Consider a **block bootstrap over time**, rather than resampling individual years.
> 
> 4. **Few clusters**  
>    A standard cluster bootstrap can perform poorly. A **wild cluster bootstrap** is often better.
> 
> 5. **Treatment assigned at unit level**  
>    Resample at the treatment-assignment level, usually the unit or cluster.
> 
> So the short answer is: **usually resample units, not years within units**. Resampling both units and years is generally not the default, because it can break the panel’s dependence structure unless done with a carefully designed block or multiway bootstrap.
